<<<
which . . That is due to the particular verb showing, which prevents applicability from including
===
*which* . . . That is due to the particular verb *showing*, which prevents *applicability* from including
>>>

<<<
(1: 14 gives
===
(1 : 14 gives
>>>

<<<
e.g. to showing how far the principles which... are applicable.
===
e.g. *to showing how far the principles which . . . are applicable.*
>>>

<<<
Similarly, in It has an area of under 100 square miles, & enjoys—o. 0.—a very heavy rainfall 'or does not enjoy', ' or endures °', &c., would be better in grammar,
===
Similarly, in *It has an area of under 100 square miles, & enjoys*—o. o.—*a very heavy rainfall* 'or does not enjoy', 'or endures', &c., would be better in grammar,
>>>

<<<
&, considering the dimness with which or otherwise now sparkles, not inferior in brilliance.
===
&, considering the dimness with which *or otherwise* now sparkles, not inferior in brilliance.
>>>

<<<
phrases as applicability o. o. are grammatically not quite indefensible.
===
phrases as *applicability* o. o. are grammatically not quite indefensible.
>>>

<<<
In He never conveyed to me any intimation that he disapproved, strongly o. o., of my conduct, we have
===
In *He never conveyed to me any intimation that he disapproved, strongly* o. o., *of my conduct,* we have
>>>

<<<
an unquestionably legitimate use, otherwise being parallel to strongly, another adverb.
===
an unquestionably legitimate use, *otherwise* being parallel to *strongly*, another adverb.
>>>

<<<
In Yesterday he was our hero, but today he is otherwise, otherwise is parallel not to an adverb,
===
In *Yesterday he was our hero, but today he is otherwise*, *otherwise* is parallel not to an adverb,
>>>

<<<
but to the noun hero; 'nevertheless grammar is not offended, because the complement of to be can be noun, adjective, or adverb, indifferently:
===
but to the noun *hero*; nevertheless grammar is not offended, because the complement of *to be* can be noun, adjective, or adverb, indifferently:
>>>

<<<
He is a hero, He is dead, He is abroad.
===
*He is a hero, He is dead, He is abroad.*
>>>

<<<
That is why Governor Sulzer is the hero (0. 0.) of a quaint election story is excusable;
===
That is why *Governor Sulzer is the hero* (o. o.) *of a quaint election story* is excusable;
>>>

<<<
yet it remains true that or villain (or other opposite of hero, according to the sense desired) would be better.
===
yet it remains true that *or villain* (or other opposite of *hero*, according to the sense desired) would be better.
>>>

<<<
The type of which so many examples were given, with o. o0. answering to abstract nouns like applicability or truth, is similarly accounted for.
===
The type of which so many examples were given, with o. o. answering to abstract nouns like *applicability* or *truth*, is similarly accounted for.
>>>

<<<
Is it applicable or is it otherwise? is sound enough English;
===
*Is it applicable or is it otherwise?* is sound enough English;
>>>

<<<
its applicability 0.0. not unnaturally presents itself as a short form of its being applicable or being
===
*its applicability* o. o. not unnaturally presents itself as a short form of *its being applicable or being*
>>>

<<<
+ otherwise; it can claim a sort of secondhand soundness; like an addled egg, it has in an earlier phase been good.
===
+ *otherwise*; it can claim a sort of secondhand soundness; like an addled egg, it has in an earlier phase been good.
>>>

<<<
To sum up, 0.0. is in grammar occasionally quite correct, often indefensible, but usually capable of a rather far-fetched justification;
===
To sum up, *o. o.* is in grammar occasionally quite correct, often indefensible, but usually capable of a rather far-fetched justification;
>>>

<<<
ottava rima.

See TECHNICAL TERMS.
===
**ottava rima.** See ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^.
>>>

<<<
ought, n., is a wrong form for nought.
===
**ought,** n., is a wrong form for *nought*.
>>>

<<<
ought, v., is peculiarly liable to be carelessly combined with auxiliary verbs that differ from it in taking the plain infinitive without to.
===
**ought,** v., is peculiarly liable to be carelessly combined with auxiliary verbs that differ from it in taking the plain infinitive without *to*.
>>>

<<<
Can & ought to go is right, but Ought & can gois wrong.
===
*Can & ought to go* is right, but *Ought & can go* is wrong.
>>>

<<<
We should be sorry to see English critics suggesting that they ought or could have acted otherwise; insert to after ought,
===
*We should be sorry to see English critics suggesting that they ought or could have acted otherwise*; insert *to* after *ought*,
>>>

<<<
or write that they could or ought to have acted. See ELLIPSIS 2.
===
or write *that they could or ought to have acted.* See ^^ELLIPSIS^^ 2.
>>>

<<<
our. 1. Our)(ours. 2. Our editorial & ordinary. 3. Our)(his.
===
**our.** 1. *Our)(ours.* 2. *Our editorial & ordinary.* 3. *Our)(his.*
>>>

<<<
1. Our)(ours. Ours & the Italian troops are now across the Piave.
===
**1.** *Our)(ours.* *Ours & the Italian troops are now across the Piave.*
>>>

<<<
The right alternatives are: The Italian troops & ours, The Italian & our troops, Our & the Italian troops; the wrong one is that in the quotation; see ABSOLUTE POSSESSIVES.
===
The right alternatives are: *The Italian troops & ours, The Italian & our troops, Our & the Italian troops*; the wrong one is that in the quotation; see ^^ABSOLUTE POSSESSIVES^^.
>>>

<<<
2. The editorial owr, like we & us of that kind, should not be allowed to appear in the same sentence, or in close proximity, with any noneditorial use of we &c.
===
**2.** The editorial *our*, like *we & us* of that kind, should not be allowed to appear in the same sentence, or in close proximity, with any non-editorial use of *we* &c.
>>>

<<<
In the following, our & the second we are editorial, while ws & the first we are national:
===
In the following, *our* & the second *we* are editorial, while *us* & the first *we* are national:
>>>

<<<
For chaos it is now proposed to substitute law, law by which we must gain as neutrals, & which, in our view, inflicts no material sacrifice on us as belligerents.
===
*For chaos it is now proposed to substitute law, law by which* we *must gain as neutrals, & which, in* our *view, inflicts no material sacrifice on* us *as belligerents.*
>>>

<<<
We do not propose to argue that question again from the beginning, but...
===
We *do not propose to argue that question again from the beginning, but . . .*
>>>
